<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anti-Fascist Council for the National Liberation of Yugoslavia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as AVNOJ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anti-Fascist Council for the National Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> (AVNOJ) was the political assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>'s Partisan movement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, first convened at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihac">Bihać</a> in November 1942. At its second session at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a> on 29 November 1943 it declared itself the supreme legislative body, barred King Peter II from returning and set out a federal Yugoslavia of equal nations. It became the provisional parliament of Democratic Federal Yugoslavia in 1945.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> on 6 April 1941, and the Communist Party of Yugoslavia organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Yugoslav Partisans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a>. King Peter II and the government fled, and the government-in-exile was weak and divided. In November 1942 the Partisans captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihac">Bihać</a> and held much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lika">Lika</a>. On 26 and 27 November the council was established there at the initiative of Tito and the Communist Party.</p><p>At its founding session it adopted the principle of a multi-ethnic federal state, without deciding what system of government would follow the war. Shortly before the session <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> added the word Anti-Fascist to the name, to stress its temporary and anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> nature, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a> had urged him to set up a body solely to liberate the country and not to oppose the monarchy. Ivan Ribar was elected its president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The council met again at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a> on 29 and 30 November 1943. It declared itself the supreme legislative body, recognised the equal standing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> in a future federation, denied the legitimacy of the government-in-exile, and forbade the return of King Peter II until the people could decide on the monarchy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> was given the rank of Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and became president of a National Committee that acted as an interim government. Stalin was enraged because he had received no prior notice, but the Western Allies recognised the Partisans as an Allied force at the Tehran Conference. In the Treaty of Vis of 16 June 1944 the government-in-exile recognised the council.</p><p>On 21 November 1944 its presidency declared the Germans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> collectively guilty for the war. About half a million Germans had lived in the country before 1944. About 240,000 were evacuated, 150,000 were deported to the USSR as forced labour, 50,000 died in Yugoslav-run camps and 15,000 were killed by the Partisans, and fewer than 56,000 remained by the 1948 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The third session met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in August 1945, and the Constituent Assembly that convened on 29 November 1945 ratified the council's decisions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The council's decisions fixed the six units of the federation and defined their borders, known as the AVNOJ borders, which separated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. They were drawn as administrative boundaries but gained importance with later decentralisation and the breakup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. In post-war Yugoslavia the second session was commemorated on 29 and 30 November as a national holiday. Museums have been established in the buildings that hosted the first and second sessions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anti-Fascist Council for the National Liberation of Yugoslavia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its political body, the <b>Anti-Fascist Council for the National Liberation of Yugoslavia</b> (AVNOJ), first met at Bihać on 26 November 1942.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Axis invasion of Yugoslavia in April 1941, the anti-royalist Partisans of Josip Broz Tito and the Communist Party avoided the royal anthem and sang &quot;Hey, Slavs&quot; instead, at both sessions of <b>AVNOJ</b>, their legislative body.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hey-slavs">Hey, Slavs</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Danon, at its first hearing, at the second session of <b>AVNOJ</b> in Jajce in 1943, where Tito was named Marshal of Yugoslavia, the audience spontaneously sang the line with Tito alone.</blockquote><a href="/songs/uz-marsala-tita">With Marshal Tito</a></li></ul></section>
